Does anyone bother with non-magic weapons? Nowadays the only non-magic
ones I bother with are the longsword or runed broadsword. All the other
ones are a waste of space, and even the two above are just to keep me
alive until I make it to an altar.

It wasn't always like this. It used to be that it made sense having so
many different weapons, because you could work out the optimal one for
your character. Now *any* ordinary weapon is pathetic when compared to a
mediocre magical one.

It's time to look at the magical weapons again. It seems to me that the
monsters are tougher just to balance Mjollnir, Stormbringer and so forth -
the four or five "elite" weapons. I can't count the amount of sacrificing
I've done until I got one of these.

I think Nethack would be more fun if the magic weapons were weaker.
Mjollnir, for instance, does about three times as much damage as the best
non-magical weapon, blinds some creatures *and* will return to Valkyries.
Sting, on the other hand, gives you stuff-all except warning. The
Crysknife used to be a cool thing - it was almost a mini-quest to get one.
What's the point, nowadays? You get a mediocre weapon that deteriorates
when you drop it.

How about if all the magical weapons were made comparable to one another -
perhaps with an average bonus of 5 distributed between hit/damage. Then,
give each weapon one special ability - Mjollnir should *only* return to
the sender, Excalibur should *only* defend, and so forth. This would also
mean that you'd have more fun deciding which special ability to pick.

I think that by using only the special abilities already programmed we
have enough for the existing weapons. Bump some of the monster-specific
ones up in power - Dragonbane should kill most dragons in two or three
hits. After all, that's its special power. It's the reason why it's worth
draggin' (cough) it around.

If these changes are implemented the game will regain a bit of its
realism. Once again we can use ordinary weapons, secure in the knowledge
that our characters are not wholly crippled. It may be necessary to weaken
monsters in compensation, but I think this is a good thing. 

